{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n13 \n \n \nIn contrast to the preceding paragraph, ACME did not use a HiBMs camera for pyrometry, but \ninstead relied on use of ACME\u2019s color DataCam for such temperature measurements.  For an \nexample of how this can be done, see the techniques described in the following papers:   \nP.B. Kuhn, B. Ma, B.C. Connelly, M.D. Smooke, and M.B. Long, \u201cSoot and Thin-filament Pyrometry \nUsing a Color Digital Camera,\u201d Proceedings of the Combustion Institute, 33 (2011) 743-750.  \nB. Ma, M. B. Long, \u201cAbsolute light calibration using S-type thermocouples,\u201d Proceedings of the \nCombustion Institute, 34 (2) (2013) 3531-3539. \n \nTFP tests were conducted with 14-micron fibers spaced apart by 5 mm for a total span of 20 mm \n(in the case of 5 intact ceramic fibers), neglecting optional translation of the array.  Tests were \nconducted using arrays with and without the platinum wire in the furthest downstream position.  \nTests were also sometimes conducted with some of the fibers broken and subsequently removed, \nleaving only three fibers in the final testing for the BRE and Coflow Laminar Diffusion Flame (CLD \nFlame) experiments and for all CFI-G testing. \n \nCIR has a second HiBMs camera, without a filter, that will be used in combination with CIR\u2019s \nillumination package for alternate measurements of the soot volume fraction.  This will be \naccomplished via an extinction technique previously employed in microgravity [3,6,26], where the \ncamera and the collimated light source (with its two fiber-coupled laser diodes) are positioned at \nopposite windows.  The images are recorded at 1024x1024 and 30 fps.  Thus far, the primary \npurpose of this combined system has been the measurement of the droplet diameter for \ndetermination of the droplet burning rate. \n \nA HiBMs camera without the Liquid Crystal Tunable Filter (LCTF) was used throughout ACME\u2019s \noperations.  In the first round of the CLD Flame experiment, it was used without a filter for flame \nimaging, but this proved to be largely unsuccessful because the camera is very insensitive to blue.  \nThrough the remainder of ACME testing, it was equipped with a red filter corresponding to the CIR \nillumination package for the rest of the ACME testing.  Unfortunately, that filter is sufficiently \nbroad that significant incandescence can pass through the filter limiting its utility for \ndeterminations of soot volume fraction via light extinction, e.g., through the technique described", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 13}}